<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4026.18">
  <POU Name="FB_LogCsvWriter" Id="{b85008c5-84dc-5f29-a8ef-2a5df33db034}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   Drains GVL_Log's 256-entry ring to a CSV file on the panel's Compact Flash.
   Phase 2 of docs/plans/log-csv-file.md. Called last in MAIN, after
   fbPersistentSave().

   WHY: the panel log is RAM only. Every entry is lost on power cycle, reset or
   download, so a fault overnight with nobody attached leaves nothing to read --
   which is exactly when the log matters most. This is the consumer the ring was
   designed for; its own comment has documented the drain contract
   (slot = nWriteIdx MOD 256) since long before anything drained it.

   Off by default. Nothing here touches the card until an operator sets
   GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM THE PLAN: no byte buffer.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The plan says to accumulate rows into an ARRAY[0..2047] OF BYTE and flush it
   with one FB_FileWrite. This writes rows individually with FB_FilePuts inside a
   single open/close instead, for three reasons that only became clear once the
   Phase 0 probe had run on the real device:

     1. THE FLASH COST UNIT IS THE OPEN->CLOSE CYCLE, not the individual write.
        Data written between an open and a close reaches the OS cache and is
        committed once. So N Puts inside one open/close costs the card the same
        as one Write of N rows -- the flash economy the byte buffer existed for is
        preserved without the buffer.
     2. FB_FilePuts + FOPEN_MODETEXT IS THE ONLY FORM VERIFIED ON THIS TARGET.
        Measured 2026-08-10 by retrieving the probe's file over FTP: a single $N
        arrives as CRLF, exactly RFC 4180, and FilePuts adds no ending of its
        own. Whether text-mode translation also applies to FB_FileWrite's raw
        buffer is unverified -- and if it does, cbWriteLen no longer equals the
        bytes on disk and the size accounting silently drifts.
     3. THE RING IS ALREADY THE BUFFER. 256 entries of it. A second buffer would
        duplicate it and add a second place for entries to be lost.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   LINE ENDINGS: EVERY ROW ENDS WITH A BARE $N. NEVER $R$N.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   FOPEN_MODETEXT already turns LF into CRLF. Writing $R$N would have it
   translate the $N again, giving \r\r\n and a corrupt file. This was very nearly
   "fixed" the wrong way on the strength of a miscounted byte total; the measured
   file settled it. Do not change it without re-measuring.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ROTATION AND RETENTION (Phase 3).
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ROTATION: files roll on a new day (back to part 1) and on uiMaxFileKB within a
   day (part 2, 3, ...), so no single file grows without bound. See FileNameFor for
   why the part separator must be '_' and not '-'.

   RETENTION: on every file CREATION, states 60-62 enumerate the directory with
   Tc2_Utilities.FB_EnumFindFileList and delete oldest-by-name until the count is
   within the cap. It runs from state 0 with no file open, never on the write path.

   THE CAP IS BY COUNT, NOT BY SUMMED BYTES, and that is a deliberate choice.
   Every file is already capped at uiMaxFileKB, so N files occupy at most
   N * uiMaxFileKB -- keeping (uiMaxTotalMB * 1024 / uiMaxFileKB) files therefore
   enforces uiMaxTotalMB, and errs toward using LESS than the ceiling, which is the
   safe direction for a disk limit. The alternative was to sum
   ST_FindFileEntry.fileSize, which is a T_ULARGE_INTEGER: 64-bit, and its internal
   shape (struct of two DWORDs versus a single ULINT) was NOT among the things
   verified in the library browser. Summing it would have been a guess, and the
   count arithmetic needs no guess at all.

   THESE SIGNATURES WERE READ FROM THE IDE, NOT REMEMBERED, and two of the
   assumptions this file would otherwise have shipped were wrong:
     * FB_EnumFindFileList has NO ePath input. Its sibling FB_EnumFindFileEntry
       does, which is exactly the sort of asymmetry that does not get guessed.
     * the size member is fileSize, not nFileSize. 'nFileSize' does appear in the
       library's string table, but it belongs to something else.
   The plan's original names for this family -- FB_FileFindFirst / Next / Close --
   do not exist in either library at all.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   NEVER FALL BACK TO \Temp\ OR THE DEVICE ROOT \.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Both are writable on this panel and BOTH LOSE THEIR CONTENTS ON RESTART -- the
   WinCE RAM object store. Proven 2026-08-10: files written there by the Phase 0
   probe were gone after an Activate, while the two on Compact Flash survived.
   A fallback to either would silently convert durable logging into RAM logging,
   reintroducing the exact defect this FB exists to fix, while every status field
   still reported healthy. A directory that cannot be opened must produce
   eState := FAILED with the error latched and visible. That is the whole point.
*)
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_LogCsvWriter
VAR CONSTANT
    RING        : UDINT := 256;         // GVL_Log.aLog size -- the drain window
    HIGH_WATER  : UDINT := 200;         // flush early rather than risk overflow
    FILE_TO     : TIME  := T#5S;
    RETRY_TO    : TIME  := T#30S;       // backoff after a failure
    MAX_SKIP    : INT   := 32;          // DBG entries discarded per scan, bounded
    // Parts per day. Zero-padded to 3 digits, so 999 is the naming limit; a day
    // that produced 999 files is a fault, not a case to keep absorbing.
    MAX_PARTS   : UDINT := 999;
    // Enumeration buffer, in ST_FindFileEntry entries. The default config keeps
    // 16 MB / 512 KB = 32 files, so 64 is 2x headroom. If the directory ever holds
    // more than this, FB_EnumFindFileList reports bEOE = FALSE and retention works
    // on the first 64 it saw -- correct but slower to converge, and it says so in
    // the log rather than silently doing nothing.
    MAX_FIND    : INT   := 64;
    // Used only when uiMaxFileKB = 0, i.e. no per-file size cap. Without a per-file
    // bound a COUNT cannot bound bytes at all, so retention falls back to a plain
    // file-count limit and logs that it is doing so.
    NO_CAP_FILES : UDINT := 64;
    HDR         : STRING(40) := 'time,severity,source,message';
    E_NOTFOUND  : UDINT := 1804;        // ADS "file not found", verified on the panel
END_VAR
VAR
    eStep       : INT;

    fbMkDir     : Tc2_System.FB_CreateDir;
    fbProbe     : Tc2_System.FB_FileOpen;   // existence test, separate instance
    fbOpen      : Tc2_System.FB_FileOpen;
    fbPuts      : Tc2_System.FB_FilePuts;
    fbClose     : Tc2_System.FB_FileClose;
    hFile       : UINT;

    // Our own read cursor into the ring. The ONLY state that must not be lost
    // mid-session, and deliberately not persistent: after a reboot the ring is
    // empty and nWriteIdx restarts at 0, so a restored cursor would be ahead of
    // the writer and would skip everything until it caught up.
    udiLastRead : UDINT;
    bCursorSet  : BOOL;

    udiPending  : UDINT;
    sFileName   : STRING(40);
    sLastFile   : STRING(40);
    sPath       : STRING(160);
    sDirNoSlash : STRING(80);
    sRow        : STRING(255);
    nSlot       : INT;
    nSkipped    : INT;

    // Part number within the day. 1 is written WITHOUT a suffix
    // (flower-2026-08-10.csv); 2 upwards carry an UNDERSCORE and three padded
    // digits (flower-2026-08-10_002.csv) -- see FileNameFor for why the separator
    // must be '_' and not '-'.
    //
    // Volatile on purpose. After a restart it starts at 1, state 10's date check
    // sets the day, and state 12 then skips forward past every part that already
    // exists -- so a restart never appends to a file whose size we do not know.
    // That is what keeps the uiMaxFileKB check exact rather than off by whatever
    // the file already held.
    nPart        : UDINT := 1;

    // The day we last wrote to, so a date change is detected against what
    // actually happened rather than against a timer.
    sLastDay     : STRING(10);

    // Set by state 31 when the open file passed uiMaxFileKB; consumed by state 10.
    bRollPending : BOOL;

    bNeedHeader : BOOL;
    bLossRow    : BOOL;         // a synthetic "entries lost" row is owed
    udiLostNow  : UDINT;
    bDirTried   : BOOL;
    bClosing    : BOOL;         // draining because bEnabled went FALSE
    bErrLogged  : BOOL;         // edge guard -- one ERR in, one INFO out

    tonFlush    : TON;
    tonRetry    : TON;
    fbTrigOff   : F_TRIG;
END_VAR
VAR
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- //
    // RETENTION. Runs only when a new file has just been created, never on the
    // write path, and only while no file is open.
    //
    // Signatures verified in the TcXaeShell library browser 2026-08-10 rather
    // than guessed -- and two of the guesses would have been WRONG:
    // FB_EnumFindFileList has NO ePath input (its sibling FB_EnumFindFileEntry
    // does), and the size member is fileSize : T_ULARGE_INTEGER, not nFileSize.
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- //
    fbEnum      : Tc2_Utilities.FB_EnumFindFileList;
    fbDelete    : Tc2_System.FB_FileDelete;

    aFindList   : ARRAY[0..63] OF Tc2_Utilities.ST_FindFileEntry;
    abGone      : ARRAY[0..63] OF BOOL;   // marked as we delete, so one enumeration serves the whole sweep

    bRetentionDue : BOOL;       // a new file was created -> sweep once
    nFound        : INT;        // entries FB_EnumFindFileList actually returned
    nMaxFiles     : UDINT;      // how many we are allowed to keep
    nToDelete     : INT;
    nOldest       : INT;        // index of the oldest survivor, -1 = none
    // STRING(255) to match ST_FindFileEntry.sFileName, which is T_MaxString. Our
    // own names are ~28 characters, but a narrower target would TRUNCATE any
    // stray long name that happened to match flower-*.csv -- and the delete would
    // then be aimed at a filename that does not exist. It would fail safe (WARN,
    // sweep abandoned) rather than delete the wrong file, but matching the source
    // width removes the question.
    sOldest       : STRING(255);
    j             : INT;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 0 -- cursor baseline, and the enable/disable edges.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //

// First time we ever run, adopt whatever the ring can still supply rather than
// starting at nWriteIdx. Someone who enables logging after noticing trouble
// wants the entries that are still there, not only the ones from now on. Bounded
// by RING because older slots have already been overwritten.
IF NOT bCursorSet THEN
    bCursorSet := TRUE;
    IF GVL_Log.nWriteIdx > RING THEN
        udiLastRead := GVL_Log.nWriteIdx - RING;
    ELSE
        udiLastRead := 0;
    END_IF
END_IF

// Turning logging OFF must not abandon a part-written flush or a full buffer:
// drain what is pending and close cleanly, THEN go idle.
fbTrigOff(CLK := NOT GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled);
IF fbTrigOff.Q AND (GVL_Log.nWriteIdx > udiLastRead) THEN
    bClosing := TRUE;
END_IF

udiPending := GVL_Log.nWriteIdx - udiLastRead;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 1 -- flush trigger.
//
// The quiet timer is the flash-wear control: entries collect in the ring and
// reach the card once per uiFlushSec, so a busy second costs one open/close
// rather than dozens. HIGH_WATER is the safety valve -- at 200 of 256 pending
// we stop waiting for the timer, because passing 256 loses entries.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
tonFlush(IN  := (udiPending > 0) AND GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled,
         PT  := UDINT_TO_TIME(GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.uiFlushSec * 1000));

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 2 -- the state machine.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
CASE eStep OF

// ---------------- 0: idle ---------------- //
0:
    // OFF means "not logging". BUFFER means "logging, entries accumulating in the
    // ring, waiting for the flush interval". Reporting OFF while enabled would
    // read as a fault on the Logs page when nothing is wrong.
    IF GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled THEN
        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.BUFFER;
    ELSE
        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.OFF;
    END_IF

    // RETENTION FIRST while idle: no file is open at this point, and a sweep that
    // has to delete several files must not sit between a flush and its close.
    IF bRetentionDue AND GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled THEN
        bRetentionDue := FALSE;
        eStep := 60;

    ELSIF (GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled OR bClosing)
       AND (udiPending > 0)
       AND (tonFlush.Q OR (udiPending >= HIGH_WATER) OR bClosing) THEN

        // OVERFLOW CHECK, before anything else touches the cursor. If more than
        // RING entries arrived since we last read, the oldest are already
        // overwritten and are gone -- unavoidable, the ring is finite. What is
        // NOT acceptable is losing them silently, so the count is published AND
        // a synthetic row goes into the file itself. A reader of the CSV must be
        // able to see the gap without also having to read a PLC symbol.
        IF udiPending > RING THEN
            udiLostNow := udiPending - RING;
            GVL_Log.stLogFile.udiEntriesDropped :=
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.udiEntriesDropped + udiLostNow;
            udiLastRead := GVL_Log.nWriteIdx - RING;
            bLossRow    := TRUE;
        END_IF

        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.BUFFER;
        eStep := 5;
    END_IF

// ---------------- 5: make sure the directory exists ---------------- //
// Once per session. Errors are IGNORED on purpose: "already exists" is an error
// from this FB and is also the normal case, and distinguishing it from a real
// failure by error number would be guessing. If the directory genuinely cannot
// be made, the open in state 20 fails and THAT is reported -- one authoritative
// error rather than two competing ones.
5:
    IF bDirTried THEN
        eStep := 10;
    ELSE
        sDirNoSlash := GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.sDir;
        IF (LEN(sDirNoSlash) > 1)
           AND (RIGHT(sDirNoSlash, 1) = '\') THEN
            sDirNoSlash := LEFT(sDirNoSlash, LEN(sDirNoSlash) - 1);
        END_IF
        fbMkDir(sPathName := sDirNoSlash,
                ePath     := Tc2_System.PATH_GENERIC,
                bExecute  := TRUE,
                tTimeout  := FILE_TO);
        IF NOT fbMkDir.bBusy THEN
            fbMkDir(bExecute := FALSE);
            bDirTried := TRUE;
            eStep := 10;
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 10: filename, and does it already exist? ---------------- //
10:
    // -- DATE ROLL. A new day always restarts at part 1. Compared against the
    //    day we last WROTE, not against a timer, so a panel that was off over
    //    midnight still starts a fresh file on the first flush of the new day.
    IF GVL_Log.sToday <> sLastDay THEN
        sLastDay     := GVL_Log.sToday;
        nPart        := 1;
        bRollPending := FALSE;      // superseded by the date roll
    END_IF

    // -- SIZE ROLL, requested by state 31 when the open file passed the cap.
    IF bRollPending THEN
        bRollPending := FALSE;
        nPart        := nPart + 1;
    END_IF

    sFileName := FileNameFor(nPartNo := nPart);
    sPath     := CONCAT(GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.sDir, sFileName);

    IF sFileName = sLastFile THEN
        bNeedHeader := FALSE;       // same file we appended to last time
        eStep := 20;
    ELSE
        eStep := 11;                // new name -- has to be tested
    END_IF

// ---------------- 11/12: existence probe ---------------- //
// A header belongs at the top of a file, once. Opening in append mode cannot
// tell us whether we created it, so we test first with a READ open: 1804 means
// absent, which is the one error code this project has verified on the panel.
// Costs one extra call per new file, i.e. once a day.
11:
    fbProbe(sPathName := sPath,
            nMode     := Tc2_System.FOPEN_MODEREAD,
            ePath     := Tc2_System.PATH_GENERIC,
            bExecute  := TRUE,
            tTimeout  := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbProbe.bBusy THEN
        fbProbe(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbProbe.bError THEN
            // Absent -> we will create it, so it needs a header and its byte
            // count starts from a known zero. Any OTHER error is left for the
            // append open in state 20 to report, so exactly one place decides
            // the directory is unusable.
            bNeedHeader := (fbProbe.nErrId = E_NOTFOUND);
            eStep := 20;
        ELSE
            hFile := fbProbe.hFile;
            eStep := 12;            // exists -- close the probe, then skip past it
        END_IF
    END_IF

// -- 12: the candidate already exists, so ADVANCE TO THE NEXT PART.
//
// This is what keeps the size cap honest across a restart. uiBytesInFile is
// volatile, so after a reboot it reads 0 for a file that may already be near the
// cap -- append to it and the file could reach twice uiMaxFileKB before rolling.
// Getting its real size would need FB_FileProperties or seek/tell, and NEITHER
// signature could be verified for this target, so instead we never append to a
// file we did not create this session: skip forward to the first part that does
// not exist yet. The byte count is then always exact by construction.
//
// Costs one extra file per PLC restart, which is nothing. Within a session the
// normal append path short-circuits this probe entirely (sFileName = sLastFile).
12:
    fbClose(hFile := hFile, bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbClose.bBusy THEN
        fbClose(bExecute := FALSE);
        hFile := 0;
        IF nPart >= MAX_PARTS THEN
            // Refusing is right: silently appending to part 999 would resume the
            // very unknown-size behaviour this state exists to avoid, and a day
            // that has produced 999 parts is a fault worth reporting anyway.
            Fail(nErr := 0, sWhy := 'too many parts today');
        ELSE
            nPart := nPart + 1;
            eStep := 10;
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 20: open for append ---------------- //
20:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.OPENING;
    fbOpen(sPathName := sPath,
           nMode     := Tc2_System.FOPEN_MODEAPPEND OR Tc2_System.FOPEN_MODETEXT,
           ePath     := Tc2_System.PATH_GENERIC,
           bExecute  := TRUE,
           tTimeout  := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbOpen.bBusy THEN
        fbOpen(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbOpen.bError THEN
            Fail(nErr := fbOpen.nErrId, sWhy := 'open failed');
        ELSE
            hFile := fbOpen.hFile;
            IF bNeedHeader THEN
                sLastFile := sFileName;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.sCurrentFile := sFileName;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile := 0;
                // A file was CREATED, so the directory grew -> a retention sweep
                // is owed. Deferred to state 0 rather than run here, so it never
                // happens with a file open or on the write path.
                bRetentionDue := TRUE;
                eStep := 25;
            ELSE
                sLastFile := sFileName;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.sCurrentFile := sFileName;
                eStep := 30;
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 25: header row, new files only ---------------- //
25:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.WRITING;
    fbPuts(hFile := hFile, sLine := CONCAT(HDR, '$N'),
           bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbPuts.bBusy THEN
        fbPuts(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbPuts.bError THEN
            Fail(nErr := fbPuts.nErrId, sWhy := 'header write failed');
        ELSE
            // +1 because text mode expands the single $N to CRLF on disk.
            GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile :=
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile + INT_TO_UDINT(LEN(HDR)) + 2;
            bNeedHeader := FALSE;
            eStep := 30;
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 30: the synthetic loss row, if one is owed ---------------- //
30:
    IF NOT bLossRow THEN
        eStep := 31;
    ELSE
        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.WRITING;
        sRow := CONCAT(CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.sNow), ',"WARN","LogCsv","'),
                CONCAT(UDINT_TO_STRING(udiLostNow),
                       ' entries lost - CSV writer fell behind the ring"$N'));
        fbPuts(hFile := hFile, sLine := sRow,
               bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
        IF NOT fbPuts.bBusy THEN
            fbPuts(bExecute := FALSE);
            IF fbPuts.bError THEN
                Fail(nErr := fbPuts.nErrId, sWhy := 'loss row write failed');
            ELSE
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile :=
                    GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile + INT_TO_UDINT(LEN(sRow)) + 1;
                bLossRow := FALSE;
                eStep := 31;
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 31: one row per pass until caught up ---------------- //
31:
    IF udiLastRead >= GVL_Log.nWriteIdx THEN
        eStep := 40;
    ELSE
        // Discard DBG without a file call. Unconditional -- independent of
        // GVL_Log.bDebugMode, which still governs the on-screen ring. DBG is the
        // 1 Hz robot keep-alive trace; it would bury the file and burn the card.
        // Bounded per scan so a long DBG run cannot stretch the PlcTask.
        nSkipped := 0;
        WHILE (udiLastRead < GVL_Log.nWriteIdx)
              AND (nSkipped < MAX_SKIP) DO
            nSlot := UDINT_TO_INT(udiLastRead MOD RING);
            IF GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].eSev = E_LogSev.DBG THEN
                udiLastRead := udiLastRead + 1;
                nSkipped := nSkipped + 1;
            ELSE
                EXIT;
            END_IF
        END_WHILE

        IF udiLastRead >= GVL_Log.nWriteIdx THEN
            eStep := 40;
        ELSE
            GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.WRITING;
            nSlot := UDINT_TO_INT(udiLastRead MOD RING);
            sRow  := RowFor(nSlot := nSlot);
            fbPuts(hFile := hFile, sLine := sRow,
                   bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
            IF NOT fbPuts.bBusy THEN
                fbPuts(bExecute := FALSE);
                IF fbPuts.bError THEN
                    Fail(nErr := fbPuts.nErrId, sWhy := 'row write failed');
                ELSE
                    // Cursor advances ONLY after the write is confirmed. If it
                    // advanced first, a failure here would drop the entry
                    // permanently and invisibly -- the one outcome this whole FB
                    // is arranged to prevent.
                    udiLastRead := udiLastRead + 1;
                    GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile :=
                        GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile + INT_TO_UDINT(LEN(sRow)) + 1;

                    // -- SIZE CAP. Checked after each row rather than once per
                    //    flush, so the overshoot is bounded by ONE row (~71
                    //    bytes) instead of by however much a flush happened to
                    //    write. Close now and let state 40 decide whether to
                    //    carry straight on into the next part.
                    //
                    //    uiMaxFileKB = 0 means NO size cap -- guarded, because
                    //    without it a zero would roll on every single row and
                    //    produce one file per entry, which on Compact Flash is
                    //    the worst thing this FB could do.
                    IF (GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.uiMaxFileKB > 0)
                       AND (GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile >=
                            GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.uiMaxFileKB * 1024) THEN
                        bRollPending := TRUE;
                        eStep := 40;
                    END_IF
                END_IF
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 40: close ---------------- //
40:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.CLOSING;
    fbClose(hFile := hFile, bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbClose.bBusy THEN
        fbClose(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbClose.bError THEN
            Fail(nErr := fbClose.nErrId, sWhy := 'close failed');
        ELSE
            hFile := 0;
            // One INFO out, matching the one ERR in. Same discipline as
            // FB_RobotTcpClient's bStateUnanswered: a repeating condition must
            // not be level-logged into a 20-entry ring.
            IF bErrLogged THEN
                bErrLogged := FALSE;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.iErrorCode := 0;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.sErrorText := '';
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := 'LogCsv',
                           sMsg  := CONCAT('CSV log recovered, writing ',
                                           sFileName));
            END_IF
            // -- CARRY ON INTO THE NEXT PART, but only if there is something
            //    left to write. Rolling unconditionally would create a new file
            //    holding nothing but a header every time the cap is hit on the
            //    last pending row. The roll request survives in bRollPending
            //    until a flush actually needs it.
            IF bRollPending AND (udiLastRead < GVL_Log.nWriteIdx) THEN
                eStep := 10;
            ELSE
                tonFlush(IN := FALSE);  // restart the quiet interval
                bClosing := FALSE;
                eStep := 0;
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 60: enumerate the log directory ---------------- //
// ONE call returns the whole list plus nFindFiles. That is what made asking for
// the verified signature worth it: the fallback design would have had to probe
// several hundred candidate filenames one at a time.
//
// FB_EnumFindFileList has NO ePath input -- unlike FB_EnumFindFileEntry -- so the
// path must be absolute, which ours is. eCmd is left at its default
// (eEnumCmd_First) deliberately: not naming the enumerator avoids depending on a
// spelling that was never verified.
60:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.ROTATING;
    fbEnum(sPathName  := CONCAT(GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.sDir, 'flower-*.csv'),
           pFindList  := ADR(aFindList),
           cbFindList := SIZEOF(aFindList),
           bExecute   := TRUE,
           tTimeout   := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbEnum.bBusy THEN
        fbEnum(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbEnum.bError THEN
            // A FAILED SWEEP MUST NOT FAIL THE WRITER. Logging still works with a
            // directory we cannot enumerate -- it just stops being pruned. Report
            // once and carry on, rather than dropping into FAILED and stopping the
            // log, which would be a worse outcome than an oversized directory.
            IF NOT bErrLogged THEN
                bErrLogged := TRUE;
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.WARN, sSource := 'LogCsv',
                           sMsg  := CONCAT('retention enumerate failed, err ',
                                           UDINT_TO_STRING(fbEnum.nErrID)));
            END_IF
            eStep := 0;
        ELSE
            nFound := UDINT_TO_INT(fbEnum.nFindFiles);
            IF nFound > MAX_FIND THEN
                nFound := MAX_FIND;     // buffer bound; bEOE says there were more
            END_IF
            FOR j := 0 TO MAX_FIND - 1 DO
                abGone[j] := FALSE;
            END_FOR
            GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiFilesOnDisk := INT_TO_UDINT(nFound);

            IF NOT fbEnum.bEOE THEN
                // More files than the buffer holds. The sweep still prunes, using
                // the ones it saw, and converges over later rotations -- but say so
                // rather than looking like it did nothing.
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.WARN, sSource := 'LogCsv',
                           sMsg  := 'log dir has more files than the scan buffer holds');
            END_IF

            // HOW MANY MAY WE KEEP. Every file is capped at uiMaxFileKB, so N files
            // occupy at most N * uiMaxFileKB -- a COUNT cap therefore enforces the
            // MB cap, and errs toward using LESS than the ceiling, which is the safe
            // direction for a disk limit. It also avoids summing
            // ST_FindFileEntry.fileSize, a T_ULARGE_INTEGER whose internal shape was
            // NOT verified in the library browser.
            IF GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.uiMaxFileKB > 0 THEN
                nMaxFiles := (GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.uiMaxTotalMB * 1024)
                             / GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.uiMaxFileKB;
            ELSE
                // No per-file cap means a count cannot bound bytes at all. Fall back
                // to a plain file limit and say so, rather than pretending the MB
                // cap is being honoured.
                nMaxFiles := NO_CAP_FILES;
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.WARN, sSource := 'LogCsv',
                           sMsg  := 'uiMaxFileKB is 0, retention limits file COUNT only');
            END_IF
            // Never prune below two: the file just created plus one predecessor to
            // actually read. A config computing 0 or 1 would otherwise delete the
            // log somebody is standing there to look at.
            IF nMaxFiles < 2 THEN
                nMaxFiles := 2;
            END_IF

            nToDelete := nFound - UDINT_TO_INT(nMaxFiles);
            IF nToDelete > 0 THEN
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := 'LogCsv',
                           sMsg  := CONCAT(CONCAT('log retention: ',
                                   CONCAT(INT_TO_STRING(nFound), ' files, deleting ')),
                                   CONCAT(INT_TO_STRING(nToDelete), ' oldest')));
                eStep := 61;
            ELSE
                eStep := 0;
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 61: pick the oldest survivor ---------------- //
// OLDEST BY NAME, which is correct only because FileNameFor makes the names sort
// chronologically -- see the underscore-separator note there. Working from the one
// enumeration and marking entries in abGone avoids re-reading the directory per
// delete.
61:
    nOldest := -1;
    sOldest := '';
    FOR j := 0 TO nFound - 1 DO
        IF NOT abGone[j] THEN
            // NEVER the file we are writing. It is the newest so it would not
            // normally win, but a config that prunes to 2 makes that margin thin.
            IF aFindList[j].sFileName <> sLastFile THEN
                IF (nOldest < 0) OR (aFindList[j].sFileName < sOldest) THEN
                    nOldest := j;
                    sOldest := aFindList[j].sFileName;
                END_IF
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_FOR

    IF (nOldest < 0) OR (nToDelete <= 0) THEN
        eStep := 0;                     // nothing eligible left
    ELSE
        eStep := 62;
    END_IF

// ---------------- 62: delete it ---------------- //
62:
    fbDelete(sPathName := CONCAT(GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.sDir, sOldest),
             ePath     := Tc2_System.PATH_GENERIC,
             bExecute  := TRUE,
             tTimeout  := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbDelete.bBusy THEN
        fbDelete(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbDelete.bError THEN
            // Abandon the sweep, keep logging. Marking it gone anyway would loop
            // forever on a file that cannot be removed.
            IF NOT bErrLogged THEN
                bErrLogged := TRUE;
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.WARN, sSource := 'LogCsv',
                           sMsg  := CONCAT(CONCAT('retention delete failed ', sOldest),
                                    CONCAT(' err ', UDINT_TO_STRING(fbDelete.nErrId))));
            END_IF
            eStep := 0;
        ELSE
            abGone[nOldest] := TRUE;
            nToDelete := nToDelete - 1;
            IF GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiFilesOnDisk > 0 THEN
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiFilesOnDisk :=
                    GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiFilesOnDisk - 1;
            END_IF
            IF nToDelete > 0 THEN
                eStep := 61;            // next oldest
            ELSE
                eStep := 0;
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 90: release the handle on the way to FAILED ---------------- //
// Reached only from Fail() and only when a handle is still open. The close result
// is IGNORED: we are already reporting the original error, and replacing it with
// a close error would lose the diagnosis that matters. What we need from here is
// the handle back, not a second opinion.
90:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.CLOSING;
    fbClose(hFile := hFile, bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbClose.bBusy THEN
        fbClose(bExecute := FALSE);
        hFile := 0;
        eStep := 99;
    END_IF

// ---------------- 99: failed -- back off, then retry ---------------- //
// Retry on a TIMER, never every scan. A bad sDir or a full card would otherwise
// produce a file call per scan for as long as the machine runs.
99:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.FAILED;
    tonRetry(IN := TRUE, PT := RETRY_TO);
    IF tonRetry.Q THEN
        tonRetry(IN := FALSE);
        bDirTried := FALSE;             // maybe the card came back
        sLastFile := '';                // re-test existence
        eStep := 0;
    END_IF

END_CASE

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 3 -- the STRING mirror of eState.
//
// Required, not decoration: the classic VISU cannot stringify an enum, so a text
// output bound to eState renders the raw number. Every enum in this project
// carries such a mirror for that reason.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
CASE GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState OF
    E_LogFileState.OFF:      GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'OFF';
    E_LogFileState.BUFFER:   GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'BUFFER';
    E_LogFileState.OPENING:  GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'OPENING';
    E_LogFileState.WRITING:  GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'WRITING';
    E_LogFileState.CLOSING:  GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'CLOSING';
    E_LogFileState.ROTATING: GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'ROTATING';
    E_LogFileState.FAILED:   GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'FAILED';
ELSE
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := '?';
END_CASE
]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
    <Method Name="FileNameFor" Id="{9f2c6b04-71ea-5c38-b3d6-0e84a5f17c62}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   Build the filename for a part number.

     part 1   flower-2026-08-10.csv
     part 2   flower-2026-08-10_002.csv
     part 3   flower-2026-08-10_003.csv

   THE NAMES MUST SORT CHRONOLOGICALLY, and everything about this scheme serves
   that: retention deletes oldest-by-name, which is only correct while a plain
   string sort puts the oldest first. Hence YYYY-MM-DD rather than any local date
   order, and a ZERO-PADDED three-digit part so that _002 sorts before _010.

   THE SEPARATOR IS AN UNDERSCORE, AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC. Part 1 carries no
   suffix, which reads better and is what is already on the card -- but that only
   sorts correctly if the separator is greater than '.'. With a hyphen it is NOT:
   '-' is 0x2D and '.' is 0x2E, so 'flower-2026-08-10-002.csv' sorts BEFORE
   'flower-2026-08-10.csv' and oldest-by-name would delete part 2 ahead of part 1.
   '_' is 0x5F, comfortably above '.', so the order comes out right:

     flower-2026-08-10.csv  <  flower-2026-08-10_002.csv  <  ..._010.csv

   Checked by generating the names for several days and part numbers and asserting
   the list equals its own sort. The hyphen version failed that check, which is
   how the byte-ordering trap was found rather than shipped.

   AN UNSET RTC GETS ITS OWN NAME, not a date. GVL_Log.sToday reads '0000-00-00'
   until FB_LocalSystemTime reports valid, and 'flower-0000-00-00.csv' would sort
   before every real file -- so retention would delete the log written while the
   clock was broken FIRST, which is exactly the one you would be trying to read.
   'flower-nodate.csv' keeps it identifiable and sorts clear of every date.

   AN UNSET RTC GETS ITS OWN NAME, not a date. GVL_Log.sToday reads '0000-00-00'
   until FB_LocalSystemTime reports valid, and 'flower-0000-00-00.csv' would sort
   before every real file -- so retention would delete the log written while the
   clock was broken FIRST, which is exactly the one you would be trying to read.
   'flower-nodate.csv' keeps it identifiable and outside the date ordering.
*)
METHOD FileNameFor : STRING(40)
VAR_INPUT
    nPartNo : UDINT;
END_VAR
VAR
    sStem : STRING(30);
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[IF GVL_Log.sToday = '0000-00-00' THEN
    sStem := 'flower-nodate';
ELSE
    sStem := CONCAT('flower-', GVL_Log.sToday);
END_IF

IF nPartNo <= 1 THEN
    FileNameFor := CONCAT(sStem, '.csv');
ELSE
    // RIGHT(CONCAT('00', n), 3) zero-pads to three digits: 2 -> '002',
    // 12 -> '012', 120 -> '120'. Padding is what keeps -002 sorting before -010.
    // '_' and NOT '-' -- see the header. A hyphen sorts below '.' and would put
    // part 2 before part 1, breaking the oldest-by-name rule retention needs.
    FileNameFor := CONCAT(CONCAT(sStem, '_'),
                   CONCAT(RIGHT(CONCAT('00', UDINT_TO_STRING(nPartNo)), 3),
                          '.csv'));
END_IF
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="Csv" Id="{1b4287c1-9ff1-538d-8e24-2268967534d3}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   RFC 4180 quote one field: wrap in double quotes, and double any quote inside.

   NOT theoretical. Robot frames reach log messages as 'SYNC:NAME=VALUE,...' and
   message text already carries commas and parentheses, so an unquoted field
   would split a row into the wrong number of columns and silently corrupt every
   downstream reader. Verified end-to-end on the panel 2026-08-10: a message
   containing both a comma and a doubled quote came back off the card via FTP and
   parsed as one intact field.

   Every field is quoted, including the fixed ones. Uniform rows are easier to
   reason about than rows where quoting depends on content, and the cost is two
   bytes.

   THE PARAMETER IS *NOT* CALLED sIn. IEC identifiers are case-insensitive, so
   `sIn` IS `SIN`, the standard trigonometric function -- and the whole POU then
   fails to parse with "'SIN' needs exactly '1' Operands" at every call site. Same
   class of trap as the Logs page that could not be named `Log`. The reserved set
   includes the trig family (SIN COS TAN ASIN ACOS ATAN) alongside the arithmetic
   and string functions CLAUDE.md already lists.
*)
METHOD Csv : STRING(200)
VAR_INPUT
    sField : STRING(80);
END_VAR
VAR
    j    : INT;
    n    : INT;
    sOut : STRING(200);
    c    : STRING(1);
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[sOut := '"';
n := LEN(sField);

FOR j := 1 TO n DO
    c := MID(sField, 1, j);            // MID(str, len, pos) -- one char at j
    IF c = '"' THEN
        sOut := CONCAT(sOut, '""');
    ELSE
        sOut := CONCAT(sOut, c);
    END_IF
END_FOR

Csv := CONCAT(sOut, '"');
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="RowFor" Id="{ae6940c9-79c5-559c-a634-3cc17c6a8310}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   Build one CSV row from a ring slot, ending in a bare $N.

   Column order matches the HDR constant. Worst case fits: the four source
   fields are STRING(8), STRING(8), STRING(20) and STRING(80), so fully
   quote-doubled they come to 18 + 18 + 42 + 162, plus three commas and the
   newline = 244 of the 255 available.

   $N and NOT $R$N -- FOPEN_MODETEXT already produces CRLF, and doubling it
   gives \r\r\n. See the FB header.
*)
METHOD RowFor : STRING(255)
VAR_INPUT
    nSlot : INT;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[RowFor := CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sTime), ',');
RowFor := CONCAT(RowFor, CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sSevText), ','));
RowFor := CONCAT(RowFor, CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sSource), ','));
RowFor := CONCAT(RowFor, CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sMsg), '$N'));
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="Fail" Id="{e43e6ecc-ad8d-5b9f-81d5-9c5aa2afc35b}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   One place that gives up on a flush: latch the error, release the handle, log
   once, and drop into the backoff state.

   THE LOG IS EDGE-GUARDED, and that is the point of centralising this. A full
   card or a bad sDir repeats every retry interval, and level-logging it would
   flush the 20-entry aRecent ring and hide whatever caused the trouble --
   exactly the defect this project already has open against
   FB_RobotTcpClient's connect-failure path. One ERR in, one INFO out on
   recovery (state 40).

   IT DOES NOT CLOSE THE HANDLE ITSELF. An earlier version did, with one
   bExecute TRUE/FALSE pair in the same scan, which starts the close and disarms
   it before it can finish -- so the handle leaks. Harmless once; but a failure
   that repeats every RETRY_TO leaks one every 30 s for as long as the machine
   runs, and exhausting the runtime's handle table would then present as the file
   blocks having stopped working. So a still-open handle routes through state 90,
   which closes it asynchronously and properly before the backoff.
*)
METHOD Fail : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
    nErr : UDINT;
    sWhy : STRING(40);
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[GVL_Log.stLogFile.iErrorCode := nErr;
GVL_Log.stLogFile.sErrorText :=
    CONCAT(CONCAT(sWhy, ' err '), UDINT_TO_STRING(nErr));

IF NOT bErrLogged THEN
    bErrLogged := TRUE;
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.ERR, sSource := 'LogCsv',
               sMsg  := CONCAT(CONCAT(GVL_Log.stLogFile.sErrorText, ' ('),
                               CONCAT(sPath, ')')));
END_IF

bClosing := FALSE;

// A handle we opened is still open -- close it properly first (state 90) rather
// than abandoning it. See the header: abandoning leaks one handle per retry.
IF hFile <> 0 THEN
    eStep := 90;
ELSE
    eStep := 99;
END_IF

Fail := TRUE;
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>
